.TH PROC 5 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
proc \- process information pseudo-filesystem
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B proc
filesystem is a pseudo-filesystem which provides an interface to kernel
data structures. It is commonly mounted at
.IR /proc .
.PP
Most of the files in the proc filesystem are read-only, but some may be
writable for process control purposes.
.SS Files and Directories
The following files are available in the root of the proc filesystem:
.TP
.B /proc/cpuinfo
CPU information: processor type, vendor, MHz, cache size, and capabilities.
.TP
.B /proc/meminfo
Memory usage statistics: total, free, buffers, cached, swap.
.TP
.B /proc/uptime
System uptime in seconds and idle time.
.TP
.B /proc/cmdline
Kernel boot command line as passed by the bootloader.
.TP
.B /proc/version
Kernel version string including build date.
.TP
.B /proc/loadavg
System load averages for the last 1, 5, and 15 minutes.
.TP
.B /proc/cow_stats
Copy-on-Write memory statistics: page faults, COW duplications, TLB flushes.
.TP
.B /proc/filesystems
List of supported filesystems.
.SS Per-Process Directories
For each running process with PID
.IR n ,
there exists a directory
.BI /proc/ n /
containing the following files:
.TP
.BI /proc/ n /status
Process status information: name, state, PID, UID, GID.
.TP
.BI /proc/ n /cmdline
Command line used to start the process.
.SH NOTES
The proc filesystem is implemented using a table-driven architecture,
making it easy to add new entries. Each entry is defined by a generator
function that produces the file contents on read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (2),
.BR procfs (7)
